  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: Mid and upper ridging centered over the \nsouthwestern CONUS and Rockies, paired with an increasingly moist \nlow level airmass, will result in hot and humid conditions over \nthe next few days, with high temps approaching the triple digits \nin some areas and heat indices over 105F and exceeding 110F at \ntimes. During the overnight, temperatures will be above average \nwith lows in the upper 70s in most areas, and possibly around 80 \nin some spots Tuesday night/Wed morning. A Heat Advisory remains \nin effect areawide, except in the upper Delta where an Extreme \nHeat Warning is in effect. These were both extended into Tuesday, \nand will likely be extended again into Wednesday with some \nexpansion of the warning possible either day. \n\nAs we are on the eastern periphery of the upper ridge, there will\nbe isolated to scattered mainly diurnal convection each day, \nproviding at least temporary heat relief for some. A few storms\nwith strong wind gusts cannot be ruled out, but there are no \nsigns of organized severe potential worthy of an outlook at this \ntime. \n\nTD 2 is located over the northeastern Gulf this morning. In spite\nof its relatively close proximity, the odds of hazardous impacts \nto our area remains very low. The upper ridge resulting in the \nheat impacts locally will expand through the week, steering the \nsystem westward. Additionally, it appears increasing shear may \ndisplace much of the convection to the south side of the system. \nBased on the current forecast track, showers could make it up to \nthe Hwy 98 corridor during the middle of this week, with \nperiodically breezy conditions and gusts in the 20-30 mph range. \nAgain however, the threat for impactful conditions this far north \nis very low.\n\nThursday through the weekend: A digging Atlantic trough will \nbring a weak surface front southward into the area, where it will \nstall and eventually fall apart. While we won't be able to turn \noff the ACs thanks to this feature, it will help to cut a few \ndegrees off high temps through the back half of the week and bring\ndewpoints from the disgusting category to a more typical amount \nof misery for July. With the front not expected to push all the \nway through, daytime heat stress levels will still remain elevated\nfor areas south of I-20 through the end of the week. /DL/",
